ROWS = [
dict(id="T167", d="RW", t="Relaxation time of the flip walk on triangulations of a convex polygon",
 s="Let T_n be the set of triangulations of a convex (n+2)-gon, and consider the flip walk on T_n: at each step choose one of the n − 1 diagonals uniformly at random and replace it by the other diagonal of the quadrilateral it bisects (a lazy version keeps the state with probability 1/2). Aldous conjectured that the relaxation time (inverse spectral gap) of this chain is Θ(n^{3/2}).",
 c="The state space is in bijection with binary trees with n internal nodes (the flip is a rotation), so |T_n| is the n-th Catalan number. A lower bound Ω(n^{3/2}) on the mixing time is due to Molloy, Reed and Steiger. The analogous question for triangulations of the sphere or of planar maps, where the walk moves on combinatorial maps rather than on polygon dissections, is different and is not covered here (Budzinski, arXiv:1611.07324, lower bound n^{5/4} for the sphere; Caraceni–Stauffer, arXiv:2001.04166, a polynomial upper bound for edge rotations on planar maps).",
 src="V. L. Alev, D. Frishberg, M. Sarantis and P. Tetali, Faster mixing for triangulations via transport flows, arXiv:2605.02067 (2026); the Θ(n^{3/2}) relaxation-time bound is called Aldous' conjecture in the abstract of arXiv:2605.02067",
 loc="Alev–Frishberg–Sarantis–Tetali, arXiv:2605.02067v2, abstract: 'The previous state of the art for the mixing time of this chain, due to Eppstein and Frishberg, was Õ(n^3), while the best known lower bound on the mixing time, due to Molloy, Reed, and Steiger, is Ω(n^{3/2}). Our relaxation time bound makes significant progress towards Aldous' conjectured bound of Θ(n^{3/2}) for the relaxation time.'",
 url="https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.02067",
 why="The flip walk is the basic local Markov chain on a Catalan family and a standard test case for comparison and multicommodity-flow methods. For two decades the upper bound stayed polynomially far from the conjectured order, so the gap between n^{3/2} and the available bounds measures how well current techniques handle chains whose state space has no product structure.",
 st="McShine and Tetali (1997) proved the first polynomial bound, O(n^5 log n) on the mixing time. Eppstein and Frishberg (arXiv:2207.09972) improved this to O(n^3 log^3 n), the first progress since 1997, using a decomposition into products of smaller associahedra. Alev, Frishberg, Sarantis and Tetali (arXiv:2605.02067) prove Õ(n^2) for the relaxation time and the log-Sobolev time, hence Õ(n^2) mixing. The conjectured order n^{3/2} is not proved. Open.",
 n="Fame borderline (my judgment): a well-known conjecture in Markov chain mixing, cited in every paper on the triangulation and associahedron walks."),
dict(id="T168", d="RW", t="Mixing time of the lozenge-tiling Glauber dynamics on domains with frozen regions",
 s="Let D be a simply connected planar domain and D_δ its approximation by the triangular lattice of mesh δ, chosen so that D_δ admits lozenge tilings. Consider the continuous-time single-flip Glauber dynamics on lozenge tilings of D_δ, which rotates each hexagon of three lozenges at rate 1 when possible; its stationary law is the uniform measure on tilings. The conjecture is that its mixing time is δ^{−2+o(1)} as δ → 0 for general domains, including those whose limit shape has frozen regions (facets).",
 c="The prediction comes from the heuristic that the height function moves by mean-curvature flow (the Lifshitz law), which gives a time scale δ^{−2} in every dimension. For the hexagon of side N (so δ ~ 1/N), the limit shape has six frozen corners and a disordered region bounded by the inscribed circle; that case is now proved (below). For general domains with facets, and for the related dynamics on domino tilings, the δ^{−2+o(1)} order is not established. Compare T046 (the Lifshitz law for low-temperature Ising Glauber dynamics) and T163 (Gaussian free field fluctuations of lozenge tilings).",
 src="B. Laslier and F. L. Toninelli, The mixing time of the lozenge tiling Glauber dynamics, Ann. Henri Lebesgue 6 (2023) 907–940, arXiv:2207.01444",
 loc="Laslier–Toninelli, arXiv:2207.01444, abstract: 'the conjecture T_mix=δ^{-2+o(1)} has been proven, so far, only in the situation where φ is an affine function. In this work, we prove the conjecture under the sole assumption that the limit shape φ contains no frozen regions (facets).' Aggarwal–Toninelli, arXiv:2605.26664, abstract: 'This was predicted to hold on fairly general domains of diameter N (on the basis of the \"Lifshitz law\" heuristic) but had previously only been established in domains such that the associated limit shape has no frozen facets.'",
 url="https://arxiv.org/abs/2207.01444",
 why="Lozenge tilings are the cleanest discrete model of a (2+1)-dimensional random interface, and the mixing time question asks whether the Glauber dynamics relaxes on the diffusive time scale that mean-curvature motion predicts. Frozen regions are the generic situation for polygonal domains, and they are exactly where the existing comparison arguments break down.",
 st="Wilson (Ann. Appl. Probab. 14 (2004)) proved δ^{−2}|log δ| bounds for a non-local lozenge dynamics in which one update can flip of order δ^{−1} tiles. Caputo, Martinelli and Toninelli proved T_mix = δ^{−2+o(1)} for the single-flip dynamics when the limit shape is affine (as cited by Laslier–Toninelli). Laslier and Toninelli (arXiv:2207.01444) proved δ^{−2+o(1)} whenever the limit shape has no frozen regions. Aggarwal and Toninelli (arXiv:2605.26664) proved N^{2+o(1)} for the size-N hexagon by a multi-scale comparison with the limit shape of a volume-tilted tiling. General domains with facets remain unproved. Open.",
 n="Fame borderline (my judgment): a recognised conjecture in the dynamics of random interfaces, known to specialists in dimers and Glauber dynamics."),
dict(id="T169", d="IPS", t="Coexistence in the Neuhauser–Pacala spatial Lotka–Volterra model",
 s="In the Neuhauser–Pacala model each site of Z^d carries a plant of type 0 or 1. With f_i(x, ξ) the fraction of neighbours of x (under a fixed symmetric finite-range kernel p) of type i, a type-i plant at x dies at rate f_i + α_i f_{1−i}, where α_0, α_1 ≥ 0 are competition parameters, and is replaced by the type of a neighbour chosen according to p. Conjecture 1 of Neuhauser and Pacala: when α_0 = α_1 < 1, the two types coexist, that is, there is a stationary distribution under which both types have positive density.",
 c="With α_0 = α_1 = 1 the model is the voter model, which clusters in d ≤ 2 and has a one-parameter family of extremal invariant laws in d ≥ 3. The rigorous results near the voter point (1, 1) are for d ≥ 3, where the rescaled process converges to a reaction–diffusion equation (Cox–Durrett–Perkins, arXiv:1103.1676). Their Conjecture 2 asks that one type takes over on the other side of the coexistence region near (1, 1). The model is monotone when α_0 ∧ α_1 ≥ 1/2 (Cox–Durrett–Perkins, eq. (1.30), citing Proposition 8.1 of Cox–Perkins).",
 src="C. Neuhauser and S. W. Pacala, An explicitly spatial version of the Lotka–Volterra model with interspecific competition, Ann. Appl. Probab. 9 (1999) 1226–1259, Conjecture 1",
 loc="J. T. Cox, R. Durrett and E. A. Perkins, Voter model perturbations and reaction diffusion equations, arXiv:1103.1676, p. 12: 'Neuhauser and Pacala [37] conjectured that coexistence holds for all α0 = α1 < 1 (see Conjecture 1 of that paper) and proved it for αi sufficiently small.'",
 url="https://arxiv.org/abs/1103.1676",
 why="The model is a standard example of an interacting particle system that is not attractive in general and whose behaviour is governed by competition rather than by a single threshold, so coexistence cannot be read off from a monotone comparison. It is also the main test case for the theory of voter model perturbations, which connects the particle system to reaction–diffusion equations and to super-Brownian motion with drift.",
 st="Neuhauser and Pacala proved coexistence for α_i small. Cox and Perkins proved coexistence near the voter point (1, 1) (Probab. Theory Related Fields 139 (2007) 89–142 for d ≥ 3; Ann. Appl. Probab. 18 (2008) 747–812, arXiv:0804.0158, for d = 2), and Cox, Durrett and Perkins (arXiv:1103.1676, Theorems 1.8 and 1.9) showed for d ≥ 3 that the coexistence boundary near (1, 1) is asymptotically sharp and proved takeover on the other side, Conjecture 2 near the voter point. Cox and Perkins proved complete convergence theorems for voter model perturbations including Lotka–Volterra models near the voter model in d ≥ 3 (Ann. Appl. Probab. 24 (2014) 150–197, arXiv:1210.0830) and in d = 2 (arXiv:2310.00812). Coexistence for every α_0 = α_1 < 1, away from both corners, is not proved. Open.",
 n="Fame borderline (my judgment): a well-known conjecture among researchers on interacting particle systems and spatial ecology models."),
dict(id="T170", d="PERC", t="The Eden model limit shape is not a Euclidean ball in low dimensions",
 s="Consider first-passage percolation on Z^d with independent exponential passage times of mean 1 on the edges (equivalently the Eden growth model, or the Richardson model). By the shape theorem the set of sites reached by time t, scaled by 1/t, converges almost surely to a deterministic compact convex set B. Show that for every d ≥ 2, B is not a Euclidean ball.",
 c="The Eden conjecture was the opposite statement, that B is a Euclidean ball; simulations in d = 2 show a shape that looks circular to the eye. B has the symmetries of Z^d and is convex with nonempty interior, but no other qualitative property of B is known for any d ≥ 2 and any non-degenerate distribution (compare T050 on strict convexity and curvature of the limit shape in general FPP, and T095).",
 src="A. Auffinger, M. Damron and J. Hanson, 50 years of first-passage percolation, University Lecture Series 68, AMS (2017), arXiv:1511.03262, Question 33",
 loc="Auffinger–Damron–Hanson, arXiv:1511.03262, Section 6.1, p. 130: 'Eden and Richardson observed by simulation that the limit shape looks circular in dimension two. However, one of the most fascinating long-standing questions remains: Question 33. Show that for d ≥ 2, the limit shape of the Eden model is not an Euclidean ball.'",
 url="https://arxiv.org/abs/1511.03262",
 why="Identifying the limit shape is the central unsolved problem of first-passage percolation, and the exponential case is the most tractable one because the growing cluster is a Markov process. Ruling out the ball in the plane would be the first rigorous qualitative statement about the shape for a natural model in low dimension.",
 st="Kesten proved that B is not a ball for d > 650,000, using Dhar's asymptotics of the time constant (Dhar 1988; μ(d) d / log d → 1/2). Couronné, Enriquez and Gerin (arXiv:1008.5069) lowered the dimension to d ≥ 35 by constructing short paths. Bertrand and Pertinand (arXiv:1705.08143), pushing Dhar's computation further, report that the Eden conjecture fails for d > 22. No result is known in dimensions 2 to 22. Open.",
 n="Fame: listed as Question 33 of the Auffinger–Damron–Hanson survey and called there one of the most fascinating long-standing questions of the subject."),
dict(id="T171", d="PERC", t="The planar minimal spanning tree scaling limit is not conformally invariant",
 s="Assign independent uniform [0, 1] weights to the edges of a planar lattice (for definiteness the triangular-lattice version used by Garban, Pete and Schramm) with mesh η, and let MST_η be the minimal spanning tree of the whole plane. Garban, Pete and Schramm proved that MST_η has a scaling limit MST_∞ as η → 0, invariant under translations, rotations and scalings. Show that MST_∞ is not conformally invariant, and in particular that it differs from the scaling limit of the uniform spanning tree. Show also that the scaling limit of the invasion percolation tree is not conformally invariant.",
 c="The construction of MST_∞ goes through near-critical percolation, whose scaling limit is conformally covariant rather than invariant; this is the reason non-invariance is expected. The uniform spanning tree limit is conformally invariant and is described by SLE(2) and SLE(8). Physicists had proposed that the minimal spanning tree is in the universality class of dense polymers, which would make its limit conformally invariant (Jacobsen–Read–Saleur, cond-mat/0403277); the simulations cited by Garban–Pete–Schramm point the other way.",
 src="C. Garban, G. Pete and O. Schramm, The scaling limits of the minimal spanning tree and invasion percolation in the plane, Ann. Probab. 46 (2018) 3501–3557, arXiv:1309.0269, Conjecture 8.1",
 loc="Garban–Pete–Schramm, arXiv:1309.0269, Section 8, p. 50, Conjecture 8.1: '(i) Show that MST∞ is not conformally invariant. In particular, show that it is different from the scaling limit of the Uniform Spanning Tree... (ii) Show that InvPerc∞ is not conformally invariant.' The abstract adds: 'However, they are not expected to be conformally invariant.'",
 url="https://arxiv.org/abs/1309.0269",
 why="Most planar scaling limits that probabilists can describe are conformally invariant and come with an SLE description; the minimal spanning tree is a natural model whose limit exists and is expected to break that pattern. A proof would need a quantitative handle on how near-critical percolation transforms under conformal maps, which is not available.",
 st="Existence and uniqueness of the limit, its rotation and scaling invariance, and several topological properties (no pinching points, all degrees at most 4, at most countably many points of degree 4, and a trunk of Hausdorff dimension strictly below 7/4) are proved in arXiv:1309.0269, building on the near-critical percolation limit (Garban–Pete–Schramm, arXiv:1008.1378). The conjecture is supported by simulations [Wilson 2004] cited there. No proof of non-invariance has appeared. Open.",
 n="Fame borderline (my judgment): a well-known conjecture of Garban, Pete and Schramm, central to the study of near-critical planar percolation."),
dict(id="T172", d="Q", t="Optimal queue-size scaling Θ(n/(1−ρ)) in an n × n input-queued switch",
 s="An n × n input-queued switch has n² queues (i, j); in each time slot a matching between inputs and outputs is served, one packet per matched pair, and packets arrive to queue (i, j) by independent processes with rates λ_ij, with load ρ = max over rows and columns of the row and column sums of (λ_ij). The conjecture is that there is a scheduling policy under which the expected total queue size in steady state is O(n/(1−ρ)), uniformly in n and in ρ < 1, which matches the Ω(n/(1−ρ)) lower bound for every policy.",
 c="The usual statement takes uniform arrival rates λ_ij = ρ/n, where the lower bound n/(1−ρ) comes from the n outputs each behaving at least like a single-server queue at load ρ. Maximum-weight scheduling is throughput optimal, and its heavy-traffic queue length is known for fixed n as ρ → 1 (Maguluri–Srikant and later work), but that limit is not uniform in n.",
 src="D. Shah, J. N. Tsitsiklis and Y. Zhong, Optimal scaling of average queue sizes in an input-queued switch: an open problem, Queueing Syst. 68 (2011) 375–384",
 loc="Shah–Tsitsiklis–Zhong, On queue-size scaling for input-queued switches, Stoch. Syst. 6 (2016) 1–25, arXiv:1405.4764, abstract: 'We study the optimal scaling of the expected total queue size in an n×n input-queued switch, as a function of the number of ports n and the load factor ρ, which has been conjectured to be Θ(n/(1-ρ)). In a recent work, the validity of this conjecture has been established for the regime where 1-ρ = O(1/n^2).'",
 url="https://arxiv.org/abs/1405.4764",
 why="The input-queued switch is the basic model of a switched network and of scheduling under matching constraints, and the question asks whether such constraints cost more than a constant factor in delay over the trivial lower bound. It was posed as an explicit open problem in Queueing Systems and has driven a line of work on scheduling policies and on heavy-traffic analysis of switched networks.",
 st="Shah, Walton and Zhong (arXiv:1110.4697) proved the conjecture when 1 − ρ = O(1/n²). Shah, Tsitsiklis and Zhong (arXiv:1405.4764) obtained O(n^{1.5}(1−ρ)^{−1} log(1/(1−ρ))) when 1 − ρ = O(1/n). Xu and Zhong (arXiv:1903.00398) obtained O(n(1−ρ)^{−4/3} log max{1/(1−ρ), n}) over all n and ρ for uniform arrivals. Ameen and Wang (arXiv:2610.37521) give, for each k ≥ 2, a recursive batching policy with expected total queue length O(n log max{n, 1/(1−ρ)} / (1−ρ)^{2^k/(2^k−1)}) under uniform traffic as ρ → 1 in the large-system regime, so the exponent can be taken arbitrarily close to 1 but not equal to it. Heavy-traffic results for fixed n (arXiv:2004.12271, 2006.06150, 2206.06504) do not address uniformity in n. Open.",
 n="Fame borderline (my judgment): a well-known open problem in stochastic networks, posed as such in Queueing Systems; less known outside applied probability."),
dict(id="T173", d="Q", t="Stability region of slotted ALOHA with four or more users",
 s="N users share a channel in discrete time. User i has an infinite buffer fed by i.i.d. Bernoulli(λ_i) arrivals, and in each slot each user with a nonempty buffer transmits its head-of-line packet with fixed probability p_i, independently; a transmission succeeds if and only if no other user transmits in that slot. Determine, for N ≥ 4, the set of arrival-rate vectors (λ_1, …, λ_N) for which the joint queue process is stable (positive recurrent).",
 c="The difficulty is the coupling between queues: the success probability of user i depends on which other buffers are empty, so the region is defined through stationary probabilities of the joint process that are not known in closed form. Stochastic dominance arguments give inner and outer bounds. Versions with non-Bernoulli arrivals, multiple receivers, or adaptive retransmission probabilities are separate problems.",
 src="B. S. Tsybakov and V. A. Mikhailov, Ergodicity of a slotted ALOHA system, Problemy Peredachi Informatsii 15 (1979) 73–87; status as in C. Bordenave, D. McDonald and A. Proutière, Asymptotic stability region of slotted Aloha, IEEE Trans. Inform. Theory 58 (2012) 5841–5855",
 loc="Bordenave–McDonald–Proutière, arXiv:0809.5023, abstract: 'The stability of such systems has been open since their very first analysis in 1979 by Tsybakov and Mikhailov.' p. 2: 'For N = 3 and Bernoulli arrivals, the stability region can be characterized [26]' and 'When the number of users N exceeds 3, it becomes impossible to derive explicit stability conditions ... These probabilities are unknown in general, and so is the stability region.'",
 url="https://arxiv.org/abs/0809.5023",
 why="Slotted ALOHA is the founding model of random multiple access, and its stability region is the most basic performance question for it. The problem is a standard example of interacting queues whose stability depends on the joint stationary law, a feature shared by many wireless and network models.",
 st="For N = 2 the region is known exactly (Tsybakov–Mikhailov; Saadawi–Ephremides; Nain, as cited by Bordenave–McDonald–Proutière). For N = 3 with Bernoulli arrivals it was characterised by Szpankowski. Anantharam (IEEE Trans. Inform. Theory 37 (1991) 535–540) gave an explicit condition under an extra assumption. Bordenave, McDonald and Proutière (arXiv:0809.5023) determine the region asymptotically as N → ∞ via a mean-field limit. For fixed N ≥ 4 no exact characterisation is known. Open.",
 n="Fame borderline (my judgment): a classical open problem in queueing and information theory, well known in the random-access literature."),
dict(id="T174", d="PERC", t="The critical value p_max of 1-independent bond percolation on Z^2",
 s="A 1-independent bond percolation model on a graph G is a random spanning subgraph in which the states of edges in any two vertex-disjoint edge sets are independent. Let p_max(G) be the supremum of those p for which some 1-independent model on G, with every edge present with probability at least p, does not percolate (has no infinite component almost surely). Determine p_max(Z^2), and determine lim_{n→∞} p_max(Z^n).",
 c="For independent bond percolation on Z^2 the threshold is 1/2 (Harris–Kesten); 1-independence allows positive correlations along paths, so p_max(Z^2) is strictly larger. Balister and Bollobás proved p_max(G) ≥ 1/2 for every locally finite connected infinite graph and determined p_max(T) for trees in terms of the branching number. p_max(Z^n) is non-increasing in n. 1-independent models are the output of renormalisation arguments throughout percolation theory, so bounds on p_max translate into thresholds elsewhere.",
 src="P. Balister and B. Bollobás, Critical probabilities of 1-independent percolation models, Combin. Probab. Comput. 21 (2012) 11–22, Questions 1.1 and 1.2 as numbered by Balister–Johnston–Savery–Scott",
 loc="Balister–Johnston–Savery–Scott, Improved bounds for 1-independent percolation on Z^n, Electron. J. Probab. 30 (2025), arXiv:2206.12335v2, abstract: 'A fundamental and challenging problem in this area is to determine the value of p_max(G) when G is the lattice graph Z^2.' Section 1: 'Question 1.1 ([2]). What is the value of pmax(Z2)? ... Question 1.2 ([2]). What is the value of limn→∞pmax(Zn)?'",
 url="https://arxiv.org/abs/2206.12335",
 why="Renormalisation arguments routinely reduce a dependent model to a 1-independent one and then need a threshold above which it surely percolates; p_max is exactly that threshold, so its value bounds what such arguments can give. The question is also a natural extremal problem over dependent measures, and the gap between the known bounds on Z^2 is still large.",
 st="Day, Falgas-Ravry and Hancock gave p_max(Z^n) ≥ 4 − 2√3 ≈ 0.5359 for all n ≥ 2, and Falgas-Ravry and Pfenninger (Random Structures Algorithms 62 (2023) 887–910, arXiv:2106.08674) conjectured equality for some n ≥ 3. Balister, Bollobás and Walters proved p_max(Z^2) ≤ 0.8639. Balister, Johnston, Savery and Scott (arXiv:2206.12335) proved 0.5551 ≤ p_max(Z^2) ≤ 0.8457 (the lower bound is (35 − 3√33)/32) and lim p_max(Z^n) ≤ 0.5847, and give a high-confidence computer-assisted bound p_max(Z^2) ≥ 0.5921. Open.",
 n="Fame borderline (my judgment): called fundamental by the specialists; known mainly in the combinatorial percolation community."),
dict(id='T175',
 x='Moved on 12 Nov 2026 to the list of open problems in mathematics generally (06_math_research/top200_general_2026_11_10, seed_from_probability.tsv): Ramsey Cayley graphs: combinatorics.',
 d='COMB',
 t="Alon's conjecture on Ramsey Cayley graphs",
 s='There is an absolute constant C such that every finite group G of order N has a Cayley graph Cay(G, S) (with S = S^{−1}, 1 ∉ S) whose clique number and independence number are both at most C log N.',
 c="By Erdős's random graph bound almost every graph on N vertices has clique and independence number at most 2 log₂ N, and by Erdős–Szekeres no graph has both below (1/2) log₂ N, so the conjecture asks for Ramsey graphs with the additional symmetry of a Cayley graph. A uniformly random Cayley graph does not always work: on F_2^n its clique number is Ω(log N log log N) with high probability. The problem of Alon and Orlitsky on self-complementary Ramsey Cayley graphs, motivated by zero-error information theory, is solved for vector spaces of characteristic 1 mod 4 (below).",
 src='N. Alon, Research problems, Discrete Math. 138 (1995) 405–411; see also N. Alon, Graph powers, in Contemporary Combinatorics, Bolyai Soc. Math. Stud. 10 (2002) 11–28',
 loc="D. Conlon, J. Fox, H. T. Pham and L. Yepremyan, On the clique number of random Cayley graphs and related topics, arXiv:2412.21194, Section 1.2: 'Conjecture 1.9 (Alon). There is a constant C such that every finite group has a C-Ramsey Cayley graph.' Concluding remarks: 'Alon's conjecture remains wide open for F_2^n and F_3^n.'",
 url='https://arxiv.org/abs/2412.21194',
 why='Explicit or structured Ramsey graphs are a central theme of combinatorics, and Cayley graphs are the natural structured candidates (the Paley graphs are the classical example). The conjecture asks whether group structure is ever an obstruction to Ramsey behaviour, and it connects to additive combinatorics through counting sets with small sumset.',
 st='Green (Combinatorica 25 (2005) 307–326) proved the conjecture for cyclic groups by showing that a random Cayley graph on Z_N has clique number O(log N); Green and Morris (Combinatorica 36 (2016)) sharpened the counting. Conlon, Fox, Pham and Yepremyan (arXiv:2412.21194) proved that a random Cayley graph on any group of order N has clique number O(log N log log N), proved the conjecture for every abelian group whose order has {2,3}-part at most (log N)^{1/1000}, hence for all abelian groups of order N for almost all N, and gave Cayley graphs on vector spaces of characteristic at least 5 with clique and independence numbers at most (2 + o(1)) log₂ N. F_2^n, F_3^n and non-abelian groups in general remain open. Open.',
 n='Fame borderline (my judgment): a well-known conjecture of Alon in probabilistic and additive combinatorics.'),
dict(id="T176", d="RW", t="The mixing time of the Thorp shuffle",
 s="The Thorp shuffle on a deck of n cards (n even) cuts the deck into two halves of n/2 cards and, for each k = 1, …, n/2, drops the k-th cards of the two halves in an order decided by an independent fair coin. Determine the order of magnitude of its total-variation mixing time as n → ∞.",
 c="Each step uses n/2 coin flips, so at most 2^{n/2} permutations are reachable per step and a counting argument gives a lower bound of order log n. The best upper bounds are polylogarithmic with exponent 3 or 4, so the true exponent of log n is unknown; the analysis is harder than for the Gilbert–Shannon–Reeds riffle shuffle because the Thorp shuffle is far from uniform on a single step and has no simple inverse description. The shuffle is used in format-preserving encryption, where explicit mixing bounds matter.",
 src="B. Morris, Improved mixing time bounds for the Thorp shuffle, Combin. Probab. Comput. 22 (2013) 118–132, arXiv:0912.2759; the problem is attributed there to P. Diaconis",
 loc="Morris, arXiv:0912.2759, Section 1: 'The Thorp shuffle, despite its simple description, has been hard to analyze. Determining its mixing time has been called the \"longest-standing open card shuffling problem\" [3].' ([3] is a personal communication from P. Diaconis.)",
 url="https://arxiv.org/abs/0912.2759",
 why="Card shuffling is the classical testing ground for Markov chain mixing, and the Thorp shuffle is the standard example where coupling and representation-theoretic methods both fail to give the right order. The problem also has a practical side through the use of the shuffle as a block cipher on small domains.",
 st="Morris (STOC 2005, math/0507307) proved the first polylogarithmic bound, O(log^44 n) for n a power of 2; Montenegro and Tetali improved it to O(log^29 n); Morris (Ann. Probab. 37 (2009) 453–477) proved O(log^4 n) for all even n, and O(log^3 n) for n a power of 2 (arXiv:0912.2759). Czumaj and Vöcking (ICALP 2014, LNCS, pp. 344–355) studied the shuffle through non-Markovian couplings. Jiang (arXiv:2610.18884) proves t_mix(ε) ≤ C d^{5/3} + O_ε(d^{4/3} log d) for n = 2^d, that is O(log^{5/3} n), against the lower bound of order log n. The order is not determined. Open. Partial claim, 14 Nov 2026: the OpenAI math release preprint 'Optimal-order mixing of the Thorp shuffle' (26 Sep 2026, github.com/openai/math), with two companions of the same date, claims total-variation mixing time of order log n for n a power of two, with a Lean formalization of the dyadic result; the order for general even n is not covered. These are unrefereed AI-written claims; this records their scope, not a check of the proofs.",
 n="Fame: called the 'longest-standing open card shuffling problem' by Diaconis, as quoted by Morris."),
]
